A||N||42||Fiero5||Front plate update! The DMV Empire Strikes Back!!||||1||
Z||000000||Fiero5||10-17-2001||11:25 PM||||Be warned! This is very, very long.<P>OK, earlier we were successful in convincing the local police here in Plainfield that the ticket they gave our son Stephen was one they might want to take back. They did and the court date and ticket was thrown away bye, bye.  Then we had the State police officer who two days later ticketed Stephen for the same reason. He also lives in Plainfield, and also who just so happens to be a friend of, you guessed it!  The very same Plainfield police officer who gave Stephen the first ticket. Gee, what a coincidence there. Duh! We went after trying to get a message to him that our son has the plate bracket and so now the plate in the nose. The State trooper had said to Stephen when he gave him the stupid ticket, that if Stephen was to get the plate where they wanted before the court date, he would throw out the ticket. The State trooper finally get's word back to us that if Stephen meets him at the court house the morning of the court hearing, he will tear up the ticket. Marian goes with him to make sure their isn't any funny stuff. Get this.. the State Trooper is LATE showing up. Funny, if Stephen had shown up late, they would have thrown the book at him. Anyway, he does apparently tear up the ticket, without even actually going outside where Stephens 85GT was parked and checking to verify the plate is not in the windshield anymore and in fact where he wanted as was the whole reason for having to meet him down there. This was kinda upsetting as my son had to miss some classes at school to meet him at the court house in Claremont that morning. Why this was more than a bit silly is that this State Trooper lives in Plainfield, only a few miles from us. He could have easily swung by our house or even met Stephen at the Plainfield Police station that the trooper is at many times and done it there. Anyway, another one down.<P>I don't hear any more from Officer Plunket from the DMV on a hearing about the front plate issue or hear any more from the local police either, so I figure since it has been a few months they might have finally realized I may have something on the vagueness of the front plate law and  decided to just let the whole thing fade away.<P>So everything is quiet for awhile and I finally get my hands on a front plate bracket for myself. I try to call this Cpl. Roberts dude on the Plainfield police force to tell him all is well in the front plate land for them now, but I can't seem to get him to call me back. Then I find out via the mail apparently why Roberts doesn't seem to have the time to call me back. I get a strange official letter this past Monday from the DMV saying that my registration on my Fiero9 (that my front plate was in the windshield) AND my sons 85GT (that has it in the nose now} is allready suspended. I received this on Monday the 15th when I got home, to late that day to call anyone at the DMV. Tuesday I call first thing when they open from work. I have two 45 minute calling cards to use as I can't make long distance calls from work. I use up the first card as I keep getting transferred around and/or given other numbers within the DMV I need to call. One girl I talk to says that it is based on a short slip from the registrations we did earlier in the year. A short slip is where the local DMV makes an error and doesn't charge you enough on your registration. One lady says it is a short slip due to an incorrect weight calculation on the Fiero. I then get transfered to another who says it is because they didn't charge me the extra fee for the vanity plate costs. Another says it has something to do with something completely different, and on it goes for over an hour. Everyone I talk to is very confused and can't actually say what it is with any certainty. They check in their computers and I can tell that something is very bizaare, as they can't seem to put a finger on why I got the letter. Late Tuesday afternoon I get a call back from an Ann at the DMV saying that she has finally tracked down what the letter I got was for. My registrations were apparently suspended because Officer Plunket (the highest ranked officer representative for the state of NH in traffic matters) filed the action based on "our continued failure to follow the front plate law". I asked her, how does he know we don't have the plates there? Did he drive up here and look? Did he have the local police check it out? I told her I knew that he hadn't done either of these, because if he had he would have seen that they were on the Fiero noses. She ignores that and goes on to say that to get the two registrations reinstated, it will cost me $25. bucks per registration. This doesn't include the $25. bucks each for the short slip situation they "think" is also in effect. She then tells me that if I can't get this cleared up by the time they close that day, my drivers license will also go under suspension. She says that will cost me over a $100. bucks to reinstate if that happens.  I asked how this could all happen without them sending out any kind of warning or  official paperwork FIRST, stating all this if I didn't comply by a certain date. I then told her that this was all a moot point, as again we both have plates in the front nose now. I also told her my son has had his there for over a month now, and even has paperwork from both the local police, the state police and the NH DMV saying he was OK. She said they don't have ANY record of any of that, including the letter they sent Stephen. She asked if I could fax that to her which i did. She got that and then tells me that they sent that document in error. I said, excuse me? What a joke! <BR>I then went on to ask her how can Officer Plunkett do all of this without me getting any notice saying anything before it will happen. I  asked her-"You know, something saying that I am in violation of such and such law, and if I do not comply by such and such a date, I will have such and such happen." She says that I must have already received that and ignored it for this to be going into effect now. I said, no, no, I never got squat in the mail, or a ticket or anything in writing at all. I asked her to check her records and see if she had a record of a letter like that being sent and maybe I just hadn't gotten it. She looked and said she didn't see anything.  I also told her that I believe that for the DMV to find me guilty without a trial, a hearing or even so much as a ticket, had to be illegal. Then adding that they are now telling me that I was going to have send all that money to be "considered" for reinstatement was nothing more than extortion. Ann said that she had talked to  Officer Plunket earlier and he had told her everything and said that if I had any further questions, to call him. She then says that Plunket told her that I would recognize his name. I told her I did, and bit my lip on telling her what I thought of him right now. I then asked Ann what can I do to get this all taken care of  without it costing me all this stupid money. After we argued for a bit more, she then said the only way to get this taken care of is to have the Chief of Police in Plainfield send/fax them in "writing" that both our plates are on the front bumper where they belong. She tells me this at a little after 4pm. The DMV closes at 4:15pm. I point this out to her, and she doesn't say anything other than good luck and goodbye kinda snobby like. I immediately call the Plainfield police number and &gt;sigh&lt; have to leave a message for the Chief with the Hanover police dispatch because he is on an accident call. I tell them how important it is for him to get back to me quickly.<BR>I call back again and now I am told he is off duty until...5pm tomm!!<BR>Well, it's now Wed afternoon and I am going to meet the Chief hopefully later tonight.<P>Though I have skipped a quite a few minor details, as if I  typed in every little thing, I would be typing into the late hours of the morning. But this is all the important info on the situation. <P>I have to assume that at this point one of the two Fieros I drive (Fiero9) is illegal right now if I drive it and maybe even Stephens also, and worse than my Fiero9 is that my license may now be suspended as of midnight last night. I can't wait to see if the local police find that out, and try and pull me over - or worse. Let them just try  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>The upside on any of this right now is that I have talked to a lawyer this afternoon and explained as much as I could in the time I had. They listened and looked at all the paperwork I had. The lawyer said he see's many errors in how the police handled things (Roberts being the main one) and more importantly and more damaging (to the DMV)  is the fact that the DMV has handled many of this poorly and very probably illegally. The lawyer said that if the meeting with the Chief tonight doesn't go as it should, I should meet with them tommorrow to see how to proceed. <P>I can see this maybe going two ways. <BR>1) It may cost me some money to make it go away by paying to reinstate my reg and license, or <BR>2) it can get really nasty real fast. I can't see that happening, but anything is possible. If it get's nasty, the lawyer is prepared to actually take on the DMV in a lawsuit, epsecially if they do not reinstate everything with out any costs involved on my part. <BR>The lawyers have agreed with me that the DMV and possibly the local police have overstepped their bounds and are making up their own laws and procedures, filing complaints and issuing orders without following any of the proper legal procedures. The lawyer said the DMV seem to have decided to take the law in their own hands as they see fit. He said they can't legally do that.<BR>We shall see how all this goes in the next few days.<P>I will keep you all posted as this all unfolds. I won the first few rounds. I blew up the Death Star, but the Evil DMV Empire has tracked me to the planet Hoth. The DMV Empire has striked back!<P>Steve<P>------------------<BR>                                   <IMG SRC="http://a1040.g.akamai.net/f/1040/759/1h/206.29.192.139/info/pics/sept11.jpg">                                 <A HREF="http://kelleyfamilyfieros.50megs.com" TARGET=_blank>http://kelleyfamilyfieros.50megs.com</A> <P>Edited for spelling<P>[This message has been edited by Fiero5 (edited 10-18-2001).]<P>Edited to take out some things that were apparently too confusing to some.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Fiero5 (edited 10-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroMaster88||10-17-2001||11:37 PM||||Wow....that sounds like they are out to get you!  I hate hearing stuff like that about the DMV and the police.  Shows how easily they can screw you over <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> and how much  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> they can be full of.  It is just not right.  I hope you can get to the bottom of this without too much trouble.  Good luck and keep us posted! <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">                  <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||13
Z||000002||Jaygee79||10-17-2001||11:58 PM||||Good luck Steve.  On a lighter note, will you be coming to the dinner on Saturday?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever3000||10-18-2001||01:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>the letter they sent Stephen. She asked if I could fax that to her which i did. She got that and then tells me that they sent that document in error.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><B>Their</B> error!  That alone should mean the case is closed.  Doesn't matter if it's "in error", they could say that about <I>anything</I>.  What if we the people use that excuse?  "Oh yeah, I blew up the police station, but it was in error."  <P>They <B>did</B> send the document, you <B>did</B> receive it, and you have proof of that.  The document is what it says it is.  They can't just go back on their word anytime they feel like it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||10-18-2001||01:54 AM||||There's one good thing about living in a densely populated area, the police have better things to do than worry about Fieros without front plates (like mine).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||SLYGUY||10-18-2001||02:58 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.printroom.com/_vti_bin/ViewImage.dll?userid=SLYGUY&album_id=35876&image_id=6&courtesy=1"> <P>God am I glad I live in California. <BR>We have the same law, but they don't seem to ever enforce it with Fieros!<P>Sly<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||10-18-2001||08:23 AM||||Jeeze, I'd have put on a front plate in the first place and there would have been no problem.<P>It sounds to me like you have been waving the red flag in front of the bull and are now complaining that the bull is charging at you.<P>New York has a front plate law, mine has been on for 14 years and I have had no car cooling problems or police problems for no plate.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||mbramble||10-18-2001||08:38 AM||||On an earlier post about this same topic you wrote "I fought the law and I won".  Whoops, maybe not..  <P>I think Old Lar sums it up better than I could ever say it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||10-18-2001||09:53 AM||||Well, you might win.<BR>But it's turned into a pissing match.<BR>I can almost guarantee you, that the cops are going to be "gunning" for you. <BR>Any_little_infraction is going to be met with "prosecution to the fullest extent..."<P>Something to think about...<BR>They've already proven that they don't mind fighting dirty (not to mention illegally). <BR>I wouldn't put <I>anything</I> past them. <BR>A narcotics setup comes to mind.<BR>Watch your back. Tell your son to watch his. <BR>Good luck. <P>Please note. The previous is not designed to be a reflection on all the good police officers out there. <BR>But I wouldn't put anything past these a$$bandits.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fiero5||10-18-2001||10:25 AM||||"On an earlier post about this same topic you wrote "I fought the law and I won". Whoops, maybe not.. <BR>I think Old Lar sums it up better than I could ever say it."<P>OK, I did mention above that I had won the first rounds with them. Their throwing out the tickets was a nice victory and will add weight to our side later on if needed. But your right, oops-I may also have spoken too soon.<P>Second, I would agree with everyone who has the same opinion as Old Lar, who says they would have just put the front plate on and be done with it if the plate law was written in a very specific and detailed enough manner as other states are.<BR>Remember that this all started as we couldn't locate a plate bracket to use. GM has discontinued the aero nose bracket. So we did the next best thing. My dad and I  also don't like the plate in the nose as it does effect air flow and cooling. You can argue that all you want, but in our experience (maybe not yours) we have seen the plate effect this.<P>I have two probelms with mentality of just doing what they say to avoid any possible trouble though.<P>Are we all sheep?<BR>"Baah", let's just do whatever we are told even if we know it is wrong.<BR>The fact that I am right and they are wrong should account for something. What kind of man (or person to be politically correct here) would I be to just bow down to these morons. I don't think anyone should be "pressured" to do something just because someone has decided to ignore the law and pick on the little guy citizen. The NH law does not say it has to be on the nose of the car. That is a fact folks. Unless you are as familiar with the law as I am, you can't have a very strong argument that I may be wrong. I have confered with a lawyer who knows the law and agrees with me. I have had others who are in law enforcement who said "off the record" of course (that's real helpfull) that I am right.<BR>But I think even more important than the fact that I am right so they can just kiss my a$$, is the fact that I teach my kids to stick up for what they think is right. I tell them to never back down or give in, as that is what makes us and our way of life stronger. Our history is full of people who decided to stick up for what was right. I do not want to in anyway say that this situation is even close to any of those in history, but there is a principle here folks. I can't look my kids in the eye and say that they have to stick up for their rights, if I myself am not willing to walk the walk.<BR>I believe that we as parents need to set examples for our kids. I want my kids to know that their father will stand up for what he believes in, no matter how small or trivial it may seem at times. I want them to look up to me. I want them to know that their Dad doesn't take sh_t from anybody. I haven't in the past and I am damn well not going to start now.<P>Besides, if I just fold and give in to these morons, what kind of message am I sending them. If I can show the police and the DMV that they can't shove people like me around and take the law in their own hands, then what will stop them from doing it to the next guy (or girl)? Someone at some point has to stand up and say - "I am as mad as hell, and I am not going to take it anymore!"<P>Know what I mean?<P>Well, I will keep you posted. If I end up in jail   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I will try and have Marian do the updates LOL<P>This is raven one, over and out.<P>This message will self destruct in 5 seconds<P>Steve<P><BR>Edited for spelling<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Fiero5 (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||mbramble||10-18-2001||10:53 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR><B>....Second, I would agree with everyone who has the same opinion as Old Lar, who says they would have just put the front plate on and be done with it if the plate law was written in a very specific and detailed enough manner as other states are....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>This is the opinion that Old Lar voiced that I agree with:<P>"It sounds to me like you have been waving the red flag in front of the bull and are now complaining that the bull is charging at you."<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||LZeitgeist||10-18-2001||10:53 AM||||Standing up for what's right is one thing. But even the righteous need to choose their battles wisely, because even being right can be overcome by a sufficiently powerful wrong. Since you now have your plates mounted to the fronts of your cars anyway, which is what they wanted you to do all along, I guess this battle was ill-chosen. <P>Sounds like they've been nice in the past, and you still weren't satisfied, so now they're through being nice. <P>Besides... a license plate placed in the windshield becomes a possible visibility hazard, as well as a possible driving hazard shoudl it slide off the dash into the driver's hands, steering wheel, etc. Evidently the lawmakers assumed that any reasonable interpretation of 'having the plate visible from the front of the vehicle' would take this into consideration, and the obvious choice would be putting the plate on the front of the vehicle. Evidently they assumed incorrectly. However, this is the very reason that society today is so overrun with frivolous lawsuits and heavy-handed disclaimers to the point that nobody is willing to take responsibility for anything. Everybody everywhere is looking for any little tiny loophole in whatever is in black and white in order to get away with doing what they *want* to do instead of doing what they *should* be doing.<P><BR>No, I'm not blaming the state of the world on the predicament you have put yourself in. But I'm also in the camp that would have just put the plate on the front of the damn car and gone on with my life. No, that doesn't make me a 'sheep' - it makes me someone with the ability to calculate what gains are worth what possible costs, and what losses may be incurred otherwise. I've also learned that the only 'best' interaction with the DMV is none at all. Being licensed to drive in any state of the US is a <B>privilege</B>, <B>NOT</B> a right. They can (and will) f*ck with you as much as they want until you end up doing what they are telling you to do anyway. They don't have to play your game in order to allow you to drive a car, but you <B>DO</B> have to play their game if you want to be allowed to drive. Their game, their rules. You don't have to play if you decide you don't like the rules. <P><BR>Good luck getting things straightened out. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||88CoupeV6||10-18-2001||10:55 AM||||Good luck Steve. Maybe you can come out of this with some extra money if they keep screwing with you.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig.jpg">    <BR>:D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||10-18-2001||10:57 AM||||This whole thing kinda reminds me of Gullivers Travels where the two nations were at war over which end of an egg should be opened- the round end or the pointy end. <P><BR>'TAINT WORTH THE BOTHER, Steve||||reg||1
Z||000014||Sage||10-18-2001||11:42 AM||||Phil:ROTFLMAO!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Steve:  Good luck with your plight.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fiero5||10-18-2001||11:53 AM||||Ok, two things here are quite obvious.<P>One, there are those who don't let people push them around and there are those who don't like conflict and just do the status quo.<P>Second, the DMV hasn't done anything legal here. Yes, it is by there rules, but right now they are not even following there rules.<BR>I drive 40mph in a 40mph zone. Why, because the law says so very specifically. Why didn't I put the plate in the "nose" of my Fiero? Because I didn't have the stupid bracket AND the law doesn't as written say I have to. If some moron nbamed Officer Plunket at the DMV says he thinks he is going to enforce it as meaning the nose, then he is re-writing the law all by himself.<P>As for the windshield thing, you should go read the other earlier threads on why the plate in the windshield wasn't restricting anyones view and the fact that it was also well velcroed down. It wasn't and didn't go anywhere. It also wasn't in violation of anything, as the poorly written law says "well secured somewhere viewable in the front of the car." It was well secured, and it was viewable from the front and it didn't restrict anyones view out of the car. BUT that doesn't matter anyway. If they truly believed that it was illegal, they should have given "me" a ticket, or a summons or something first. They didn't, so they are waaay in the wrong.<P>The world is full of sheep, which is partly why the world is the way it is today. It seems like more and more these days,  nobody wants to stir up trouble, even if they are in the right. What a sad way to live. Just turn your heads and look the other way. <BR>What a sad world we live in at times.<P>When I go to bed at night, I will sleep well even if it is in a jail cell. Why? Because I was man enough and had the guts to stand up for my rights. It's true that our license is a priviledge and not a right. But the DMV can't disobey their own rules and in the process violate our civil rights and walk all over us, just because one or two of them think they are above the law. The United States of America doesn't work that way. At least not yet.<P>Hello people, wake up!<p>[This message has been edited by Fiero5 (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000016||mbramble||10-18-2001||12:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR><B>...  So what does everyone think? ...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Seems to me that you don't really want to know what 'everyone' thinks, just those who agree with you.  The rest of us are "sheep" who are "partly why the world is the way it is today".  You have a mighty high opinion of your opinion.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Raydar||10-18-2001||12:54 PM||||Hey, more power to you. I admire your determination.<BR>My only point was that this could get really ugly. Especially if the cops decide they want to really smack you down. You (or your son) wouldn't be the first person who was ever set up by a corrupt cop. <BR>It ain't paranoia if they're really out to get you.<P>Good luck. Sue the bastards. <BR>Hope you have lots of funds available. I don't think it's gonna be cheap.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||14
Z||000018||LZeitgeist||10-18-2001||01:02 PM||||Hmmmmm... let's see...<P>You didn't want the plates on the front of your cars, so you ignored repeated warnings from officers of the law (and prompted your kid(s) to ignore the warnings, too) to adhere to the spirit of the law, which you fought against by attempting to utilize the letter of the law in your defense. <P>However, now, instead of:<P>A - going ahead and mounting the plates on the fronts of your cars, <BR>B - being slightly disgruntled at the spirit of the law and <BR>C - moving on with your life, <P>you now have <P>A - the plates mounted on the fronts of your cars,<BR>B - scads of legal documentation to fill out,<BR>C - extra money you are required to pay in order to get the paperwork straightened out,<BR>D - possible points against not only your driving record, but that of your kid(s) who happened to follow your advice, and <BR>E - you have the entire local law enforcement community 'gunning' for you, AND your kid(s), AND probably your spouse, just waiting for any of you to make *any* infraction whatsoever so they can cite them or you for something that they can surely enforce.<P><BR>Wow. Yup, that's a well chosen battle, all right. But hey, anything for the price of being 'right', hmm?<P>I'm glad Colin Powell has more battle wisdom than you seem to. <P>You had somewhat of a 'pity vote' from me up to the point that I realized that you hadn't learned a thing from this whole ordeal you've put yourself (and your family) through. Not any more. C'est la vie. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||LZeitgeist||10-18-2001||01:04 PM||||Oh, I forgot to add - <P>F - lawyer bills you wouldn't have otherwise had, and<BR>G - a helluva lot of stress and headache.<P><BR>Good luck in court... I'm sure you'll need it. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||stsmithgt||10-18-2001||01:45 PM||||Small towns suck when it comes to small infractions like missing a front plate.  I've got paper work to fill out to get new plates since my car has never had one installed before.  The original owner didn't have a front plate to give me when I bought the car so NOW I have to take care of it.  It was bound to happen, getting the ticket, so it's time to pay the piper(DMV).  It sounds like the FORCE you're dealing with it more difficult than what I have to face. (I hope)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    GOOD LUCK on your adventure! <BR>oops pics too big.....editing.....<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/playmoney1.jpg"> <BR>Hmmmmm. What's still missing?????   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 10-18-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||13
Z||000021||rogergarrison||10-18-2001||02:06 PM||||I had a problem with Gahanna (columbus suburb) for a long time on all kinds of stupid issues. My tag was below my bumper (?), I didnt have my belt buckeled stopped in a mall parking lot, etc. I went to mayors office and demanded to see the mayor. After he and the police chief met Ive had no more problems there. When you get to DMV thats state issure. Make appointment with governor or call a tv station. Take all your paperwork with you. Gov works for YOU, so you have every right to demand an appointment.||||reg||1
Z||000022||falconhulk||10-18-2001||02:15 PM||||I dont care about this argument but I have always been PI$$ED about that statement "Driving is a privilage not a right".  Ok so I guess that walking is a privilage not a right?  Well you might say hey the state/city/fed govt own the roads?  Those same govt's own the sidewalks also.  That statement angers me to no end.  I pay taxes, I pay tolls, I obey all laws.  I do this becasue its right not becasue I want to keep my "privilage".  Its like being treated like a kid.  You might say "Well a lot of drivers are bad and need to be treated like kids".  My response is that the people giving you this "privilage" are idiots themselves!!  It amazes me how you can drive with someone and they are constantly saying how stupid everyone else is and what fools they are. These fools are the ones controlling everything in the country!  The guy that cut you off might work for the DMV the jerk that ran you off the road might be the chief of police in plain cloths.  These people are not above us, they are us!  Would you put your 8 year old son in charge of a whole school?  Sorry about this but that one statement makes me want to move off in to the woods or something........hey wait a minute I already live in the woods! ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Tigger||10-18-2001||02:51 PM||||I'm sure glad they don't have a front plate law here in Michigan because I would staple the front plate to my forehead before I would put it on the front of my Fiero.<P>Man, that would hurt so     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>j/k, I really wouldn't go that far.<P>Well...<P>No, I wouldn't go that far.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||LZeitgeist||10-18-2001||03:18 PM||||If driving a motor vehicle on public roadways was a "right", then there would be no way to prevent those who consistently break the law and/or endanger others (not you Fiero5 - I'm speaking in general terms here) to be removed from the midst of the rest of the safe, law-abiding drivers.<P>Driving is a privilege because it has been proven that some people do not possess the sense to use vehicles with safety and concern for others. <P>Here's a free clue, falconhulk - keep the dollar: That which you rail so flailingly against keeps *you* safe. <P>||||reg||1
